{
 "duration": 57.0,
 "captions": [
  {
   "start": 0.2,
   "text": "Your gas station's tanks are full.",
   "end": 2.6
  },
  {
   "start": 2.6,
   "text": "The pumps are dead.",
   "end": 4.1
  },
  {
   "start": 4.1,
   "text": "Why can't you buy one drop?",
   "end": 6.07
  },
  {
   "start": 6.07,
   "text": "And why did one state write a law about it?",
   "end": 9.02
  },
  {
   "start": 9.02,
   "text": "The gas sits in a tank underground.",
   "end": 11.48
  },
  {
   "start": 11.48,
   "text": "An electric pump lifts it.",
   "end": 13.39
  },
  {
   "start": 13.39,
   "text": "No power, no pump.",
   "end": 14.77
  },
  {
   "start": 14.77,
   "text": "The card reader's dead too.",
   "end": 16.75
  },
  {
   "start": 16.75,
   "text": "So why not just keep a generator?",
   "end": 19.09
  },
  {
   "start": 19.09,
   "text": "After two brutal hurricane seasons, Florida passed a law.",
   "end": 22.81
  },
  {
   "start": 22.81,
   "text": "It doesn't make every station buy one.",
   "end": 25.45
  },
  {
   "start": 25.45,
   "text": "It makes many generator-ready.",
   "end": 27.61
  },
  {
   "start": 27.61,
   "text": "Big stations near interstates, and every one built since two thousand six, must be wired so a generator plugs right in.",
   "end": 35.02
  },
  {
   "start": 35.02,
   "text": "Only a few states, like Florida, Louisiana and New York, do this.",
   "end": 39.1
  },
  {
   "start": 39.1,
   "text": "But in a big storm, tanks run dry.",
   "end": 41.44
  },
  {
   "start": 41.44,
   "text": "During Irma, GasBuddy found fifty to seventy percent of stations empty in some Florida cities.",
   "end": 47.46
  },
  {
   "start": 47.46,
   "text": "The gas you can count on is in your car.",
   "end": 50.22
  },
  {
   "start": 50.22,
   "text": "It doesn't need the grid.",
   "end": 52.08
  },
  {
   "start": 52.08,
   "text": "Ready.gov says half a tank, always.",
   "end": 54.72
  },
  {
   "start": 54.72,
   "text": "Full if evacuation looks likely.",
   "end": 57.0
  }
 ],
